WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
Language modeling is one of

2
00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.930
the most basic and
important task

3
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.910
in natural language processing.

4
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.400
It is also one that
RNNs do very well.

5
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.715
In this video, you'll
learn about how to

6
00:00:11.715 --> 00:00:14.475
build a language
model using an RNN,

7
00:00:14.475 --> 00:00:16.090
and this will lead up toward

8
00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.540
a fun programming exercise
at the end of this week,

9
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.300
where you build a language
model and use it to

10
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.240
generate Shakespeare like texts
and other types of texts.

11
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.695
Let's get started.

12
00:00:25.695 --> 00:00:27.915
What is a language model?

13
00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:30.195
Let's say you're building a
speech recognition system

14
00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:31.560
and you hear the sentence,

15
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.085
the apple and pear
salad was delicious.

16
00:00:35.085 --> 00:00:37.395
What did you just hear me say?

17
00:00:37.395 --> 00:00:40.025
Did I say the apple
and pair salad?

18
00:00:40.025 --> 00:00:44.465
Or did I say the
apple and pear salad?

19
00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:48.110
You probably think
the second sentence

20
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.520
is much more likely.

21
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.200
In fact, that's
what a good speech

22
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.285
recognition system would output,

23
00:00:53.285 --> 00:00:57.400
even though these two sentences
sound exactly the same.

24
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.825
The way a speech
recognition system

25
00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:02.390
picks the second
sentence is by using

26
00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.850
a language model which
tells it what is

27
00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:08.060
the probability of either
of these two sentences.

28
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.130
For example, a language model

29
00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.690
might say that the chance of

30
00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.990
the first sentences is 3.2
by 10 to the negative 13,

31
00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.040
and the chance of the
second sentence is say

32
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.225
5.7 by 10 to the negative 10,

33
00:01:22.225 --> 00:01:25.040
and so with these probabilities,

34
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.070
the second sentence is
much more likely by over

35
00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:32.160
a factor of 10^3 compared
to the first sentence,

36
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.275
and that's why a speech
recognition system

37
00:01:34.275 --> 00:01:36.595
will pick the second choice.

38
00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:38.870
What a language model does is,

39
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.330
given any sentence,

40
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.620
its job is to tell you what is

41
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:46.505
the probability of that
particular sentence,

42
00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:49.565
and by probability
of sentence, I mean,

43
00:01:49.565 --> 00:01:52.010
if you were to pick up
a random newspaper,

44
00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.120
open a random email,

45
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.335
or pick a random webpage,

46
00:01:54.335 --> 00:01:56.765
or listen to the next
thing someone says,

47
00:01:56.765 --> 00:01:57.965
the friend of you says,

48
00:01:57.965 --> 00:01:59.870
what is the chance
that the next sentence

49
00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.100
you read somewhere out there in

50
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.315
the world will be

51
00:02:02.315 --> 00:02:06.240
a particular sentence like
the apple and pear salad?

52
00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.670
This is a fundamental
component for

53
00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.070
both speech recognition
systems as you've just seen,

54
00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.130
as well as for machine
translation systems,

55
00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.275
where translation
systems want to output

56
00:02:18.275 --> 00:02:21.385
only sentences that are likely.

57
00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:25.340
So the basic job of a
language model is to input

58
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.810
the sentence which I'm going
to write as a sequence y^1,

59
00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.265
y^2 up to y^ty,

60
00:02:33.265 --> 00:02:35.390
and for language model,

61
00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:38.600
it'll be useful to
represent the sentences

62
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.140
as outputs y rather
than as inputs x.

63
00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.530
But what a language
model does is it

64
00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.210
estimates the probability of

65
00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.110
that particular
sequence of words.

66
00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.610
How do you build
a language model?

67
00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.160
To build such a
model using a RNN,

68
00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.020
you will first need a training

69
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.435
set comprising a large corpus of

70
00:03:05.435 --> 00:03:07.670
English text or text from

71
00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.820
whatever language you want to
build a language model of.

72
00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:14.510
The word corpus is an NLP
terminology that just means

73
00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:19.220
a large body or a very large
tens of English sentences.

74
00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.975
Let's say you get

75
00:03:20.975 --> 00:03:23.110
a sentence in your
training set as follows,

76
00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:25.070
cats average 15 hours
of sleep a day.

77
00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:29.985
The first thing you would do
is tokenize the sentence,

78
00:03:29.985 --> 00:03:31.475
and that means you would form

79
00:03:31.475 --> 00:03:35.195
a vocabulary as we saw
in an earlier video,

80
00:03:35.195 --> 00:03:39.170
and then map each of
these words to say

81
00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:43.910
one-hot vectors or to
indices in your vocabulary.

82
00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.890
One thing you might
also want to do

83
00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.095
is model when sentences end.

84
00:03:48.095 --> 00:03:50.990
So another common
thing to do is to add

85
00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:52.935
an extra token called

86
00:03:52.935 --> 00:03:56.265
EOS that stands for
end of sentence,

87
00:03:56.265 --> 00:04:00.185
that can help you figure
out when a sentence ends.

88
00:04:00.185 --> 00:04:01.550
We'll talk more
about this later.

89
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.350
But the EOS token can be

90
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.840
appended to the end
of every sentence in

91
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.850
your training set if
you want your model to

92
00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:11.065
explicitly capture
when sentences end.

93
00:04:11.065 --> 00:04:13.965
We won't use the
end-of-sentence token

94
00:04:13.965 --> 00:04:16.400
for the problem exercise
at the end of this week.

95
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:17.930
But for some applications,

96
00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.660
you might want to use this,

97
00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.610
and we'll see later where
this comes in handy.

98
00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.175
In this example, we have y^1,

99
00:04:25.175 --> 00:04:28.330
y^2, y^3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9.

100
00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.600
Nine inputs in this
example if you

101
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.075
append the end of sentence
token to the end.

102
00:04:33.075 --> 00:04:34.820
Doing the tokenization step,

103
00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:36.230
you can decide whether or not

104
00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.180
the period should
be a token as well.

105
00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.090
In this example, I'm just
ignoring punctuation,

106
00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:42.860
so I'm just using day

107
00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.080
as another token and
omitting the period.

108
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.420
If you want to treat the period

109
00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.090
or other punctuation
as the explicit token,

110
00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:53.830
then you could add the period
to your vocabulary as well.

111
00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.105
Now, one other detail would be,

112
00:04:56.105 --> 00:04:57.440
what if some of the words in

113
00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.590
your training set are
not in your vocabulary?

114
00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.875
If your vocabulary
uses 10,000 words,

115
00:05:02.875 --> 00:05:05.330
maybe the 10,000 most
common words in English,

116
00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:07.790
then the term Mau as a decision,

117
00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:09.860
Mau's breed of cat,
that might not be

118
00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:12.575
in one of your top
10,000 tokens.

119
00:05:12.575 --> 00:05:15.440
In that case, you could take
the word Mau and replace

120
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.650
it with a unique
token called UNK,

121
00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.470
which stands for unknown words,

122
00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.640
and we just model the chance of

123
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.400
the unknown word instead
of the specific word, Mau.

124
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:29.300
Having carried out the
tokenization step,

125
00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:30.620
which basically means taking

126
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.390
the input sentence
and map here to

127
00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:33.530
the individual tokens or

128
00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:36.095
the individual words
in your vocabulary,

129
00:05:36.095 --> 00:05:38.120
next, let's build an RNN to

130
00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.140
model the chance of these
different sequences.

131
00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.370
One of the things we'll see on

132
00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.620
the next slide is that
you end up setting

133
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:51.440
the inputs x^t to be
equal to y of t minus 1.

134
00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.350
But you'll see that
in a little bit.

135
00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:55.710
Let's go on to build
the RNN model,

136
00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.020
and I'm going to continue to use

137
00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:00.550
this sentence as the
running example.

138
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:03.940
This will be the
RNN architecture.

139
00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.180
At time zero,

140
00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.320
you're going to end up computing

141
00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.715
some activation a_1 as a
function of some input x_1,

142
00:06:13.715 --> 00:06:19.930
and x_1 would just be
set to zero vector.

143
00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.820
The previous a_0 by convention,

144
00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.325
also set that to vector zeros.

145
00:06:25.325 --> 00:06:27.845
But what a_1 does
is it will make

146
00:06:27.845 --> 00:06:30.450
a Softmax prediction to try to

147
00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:36.455
figure out what is the
probability of the first word y,

148
00:06:36.455 --> 00:06:40.210
so that's going to be y_1.

149
00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:44.020
What this step does is
really it has a Softmax,

150
00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.630
so it's trying to
predict what is

151
00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.930
the probability of any
word in a dictionary,

152
00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.590
what's the chance that
the first word is a,

153
00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:53.445
what's the chance that
the first word is Aaron,

154
00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:58.805
and then what's the chance
that the first word is cats,

155
00:06:58.805 --> 00:07:00.410
all the way up to
what's the chance

156
00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:01.725
the first word is Zulu,

157
00:07:01.725 --> 00:07:03.070
or what's the chance that

158
00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.015
the first word is
an unknown word,

159
00:07:05.015 --> 00:07:06.965
or what's the chance that

160
00:07:06.965 --> 00:07:08.500
the first words is in

161
00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:11.290
a sentence though or
shouldn't happen really.

162
00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:15.820
Y hat 1 is output
according to a Softmax,

163
00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:17.375
it just predicts
what's the chance that

164
00:07:17.375 --> 00:07:19.940
the first word being
whatever it ends up being.

165
00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.090
In our example, one of
the bigger the word cats.

166
00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:26.515
This would be a 10,000
way Softmax output.

167
00:07:26.515 --> 00:07:30.430
If you have 10,000 word
vocabulary or 10,002,

168
00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:32.210
I guess you can't
unknown word and

169
00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:35.375
the sentence has two
additional tokens.

170
00:07:35.375 --> 00:07:38.500
Then the RNN steps forward to

171
00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.415
the next step and has

172
00:07:40.415 --> 00:07:43.265
some activation a_2
in the next step.

173
00:07:43.265 --> 00:07:45.550
At this step, it's
job is to try to

174
00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:48.205
figure out what is
the second word.

175
00:07:48.205 --> 00:07:54.500
But now we will also give
it the correct first word.

176
00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.229
We'll tell it that G in reality,

177
00:07:57.229 --> 00:07:59.975
the first word was
actually cats,

178
00:07:59.975 --> 00:08:01.260
so that's y_1,

179
00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.185
so tell it cats.

180
00:08:03.185 --> 00:08:10.160
This is why y_1 is equal to x_2.

181
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:11.645
At the second step,

182
00:08:11.645 --> 00:08:14.490
the output is again
predicted by Softmax,

183
00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:16.510
the RNN's job is
to predict what's

184
00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.760
the chance of it being
whatever word it is,

185
00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.230
is it A or Aaron,

186
00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:21.580
or cats or Zulu,

187
00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:23.650
or unknown word or
EOS or whatever,

188
00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:26.950
given what had come previously.

189
00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.130
In this case, I guess
the right answer was

190
00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.975
average since the sentence
starts with cats average.

191
00:08:32.975 --> 00:08:35.200
Then you go on to
the next step of

192
00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.605
the RNN where you
now compute a_3.

193
00:08:39.605 --> 00:08:42.755
But to predict what is the
third word which is 15,

194
00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:44.860
we can now give it
the first two words.

195
00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.530
We're going to tell cats
average of the first two words.

196
00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.130
This next input here,

197
00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:54.180
x_3 will be equal to y_2,

198
00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.920
so the word average is
input and its job is to

199
00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:00.175
figure out what is the
next word in the sequence.

200
00:09:00.175 --> 00:09:02.265
Another was trying to figure
out what is the probability

201
00:09:02.265 --> 00:09:04.745
of any words in the
dictionary given that

202
00:09:04.745 --> 00:09:09.960
what just came before
was cats average?

203
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:13.850
In this case, the right
answer is 15 and so on.

204
00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:16.280
Until at the end,

205
00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:20.660
you end up at I guess
time step nine,

206
00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.000
you end up feeding

207
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:30.955
it x_9 which is equal to
y_8 which is the word day.

208
00:09:30.955 --> 00:09:38.650
Then this has a_9 and its
job is to open y hat nine,

209
00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:40.770
and this happens to
be the EOS tokens.

210
00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.845
What's the chance
of whatever it is

211
00:09:42.845 --> 00:09:45.400
given everything
that's come before?

212
00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:47.615
Hopefully you'll predict
that there's a high chance

213
00:09:47.615 --> 00:09:50.835
of EOS in the sentence token.

214
00:09:50.835 --> 00:09:53.555
But so, each step in
the RNN will look at

215
00:09:53.555 --> 00:09:56.570
some set of preceding
words such as,

216
00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.325
given the first three words,

217
00:09:58.325 --> 00:10:01.185
what is the distribution
over the next word?

218
00:10:01.185 --> 00:10:03.485
This RNN learns to predict

219
00:10:03.485 --> 00:10:06.365
one word at a time going
from left to right.

220
00:10:06.365 --> 00:10:08.375
Next, to train this
through a network,

221
00:10:08.375 --> 00:10:11.105
we're going to define
the cost function.

222
00:10:11.105 --> 00:10:12.965
At a certain time t,

223
00:10:12.965 --> 00:10:16.040
if the true word was yt

224
00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:20.610
and your network Softmax
predicted some y hat t,

225
00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:23.165
then this is the
Softmax loss function

226
00:10:23.165 --> 00:10:24.905
that you'll already
be familiar with,

227
00:10:24.905 --> 00:10:28.055
and then the overall loss
is just the sum over

228
00:10:28.055 --> 00:10:29.920
all time steps of the losses

229
00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.585
associated with the
individual predictions.

230
00:10:32.585 --> 00:10:35.455
If you train this RNN on
a large training set,

231
00:10:35.455 --> 00:10:37.135
what it will be able to do is,

232
00:10:37.135 --> 00:10:40.350
given any initial
set of words such as

233
00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:43.965
cats average 15 or cats
average 15 hours of,

234
00:10:43.965 --> 00:10:47.905
it can predict what is the
chance of the next word.

235
00:10:47.905 --> 00:10:52.610
Given a new sentence, say y_1,

236
00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:54.955
y_2, y_3,

237
00:10:54.955 --> 00:10:57.925
with just three words
for simplicity,

238
00:10:57.925 --> 00:11:00.190
the way you can figure
out what is the chance of

239
00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:02.290
this entire sentence
would be, well,

240
00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:05.770
the first Softmax tells you
what's the chance of y_1,

241
00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.065
that would be this first output.

242
00:11:08.065 --> 00:11:10.780
Then the second one
can tell you what's

243
00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:14.750
the chance of p
of y_2 given y_1.

244
00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:18.725
Then the third one tells you
what's the chance of y_3

245
00:11:18.725 --> 00:11:23.835
given y_1 and y_2,

246
00:11:23.835 --> 00:11:27.880
and so it's by multiplying out
these three probabilities.

247
00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.045
You see much more of

248
00:11:29.045 --> 00:11:31.295
the details of this in
[inaudible] exercise,

249
00:11:31.295 --> 00:11:34.195
it's by multiplying out these
three that you end up with

250
00:11:34.195 --> 00:11:39.200
the probability of this
three words sentence.

251
00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:42.010
That's the basic
structure of how you can

252
00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:45.050
train a language
model using an RNN.

253
00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:46.950
If some of these
ideas still seem a

254
00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.045
little bit abstract,
